# Hostinger VPS Deployment Guide

This guide details how to deploy your **Next.js Frontend** and **Java (Spring Boot) Backend** on a Hostinger VPS (Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 recommended).

> [!NOTE]
> Ensure you have an active Hostinger VPS plan and SSH access.

## 1. Server Setup (First Time Only)

SSH into your server:
```bash
ssh root@your_vps_ip
```

### Install Prerequisites (Node.js, Java, Nginx)
```bash
# Update system
apt update && apt upgrade -y

# Install Java 17 (Required for Backend)
apt install openjdk-17-jdk -y

# Install Node.js 20 (Required for Frontend)
curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | bash -
apt install -y nodejs

# Install Nginx (Web Server / Reverse Proxy)
apt install nginx -y

# Install PM2 (Process Manager for Node.js)
npm install -g pm2
```

---

## 2. Backend Deployment

### Prepare Artifacts
Upload your built JAR file ([backend/target/backend-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar](file:///c:/Users/DELL/OneDrive/Desktop/Momentum/backend/target/backend-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar)) to the VPS, e.g., `/var/www/momentum-backend/app.jar`.

### Run Backend
You can run the JAR file directly, but it's better to create a system service or use PM2 (if configured for java, but standard `java -jar` service is cleaner).

**Option A: Run with systemd (Recommended for production)**
1. Create service file: `nano /etc/systemd/system/backend.service`
2. Paste content:
```ini
[Unit]
Description=Momentum Backend
After=network.target

[Service]
User=root
# Adjust memory limits as needed
ExecStart=/usr/bin/java -jar /var/www/momentum-backend/app.jar
SuccessExitStatus=143
Restart=always
RestartSec=10
WorkingDirectory=/var/www/momentum-backend

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```
3. Start service:
```bash
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable backend
systemctl start backend
```

**Check Status**: `systemctl status backend`

---

## 3. Frontend Deployment

### Prepare Artifacts
Upload the following files/folders from your local `frontend` directory to `/var/www/momentum-frontend`:
- `.next/` (folder)
- `public/` (folder)
- [package.json](file:///c:/Users/DELL/OneDrive/Desktop/Momentum/frontend/package.json)
- [next.config.mjs](file:///c:/Users/DELL/OneDrive/Desktop/Momentum/frontend/next.config.mjs)
- `.env` (Create this file with your production environment variables)

### Install Dependencies
```bash
cd /var/www/momentum-frontend
npm install --production
```

### Configure Environment (.env)
Ensure `.env` contains:
```env
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=your_email@...
GOOGLE_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----..."
GOOGLE_SHEET_ID=...
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.yourdomain.com # or /api/v1 if using path routing
```

### Start with PM2
```bash
pm2 start npm --name "frontend" -- start
pm2 save
pm2 startup
```

---

## 4. Nginx Configuration (Reverse Proxy)

Configure Nginx to route traffic to the Frontend (Port 3000) and Backend (Port 8080).

Edit config: `nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/default` (or create a new file)

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name yourdomain.com www.yourdomain.com;

    # Frontend (Next.js)
    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection 'upgrade';
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;
    }

    # Backend (Java API)
    # Option 1: Map to /api Path
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8080/; 
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    }
}
```

**Restart Nginx**:
```bash
nginx -t
systemctl restart nginx
```

> [!TIP]
> Use **Certbot** to enable HTTPS (SSL):
> `apt install certbot python3-certbot-nginx -y`
> `certbot --nginx -d yourdomain.com`

---

## 5. Summary of Ports
- **Frontend**: Runs on `localhost:3000`
- **Backend**: Runs on `localhost:8080` (Default Spring Boot port, verify in [application.properties](file:///c:/Users/DELL/OneDrive/Desktop/Momentum/backend/src/main/resources/application.properties))
- **Nginx**: Listens on `80` (HTTP) / `443` (HTTPS) and forwards traffic.
